---
import '../styles/legal.css';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteChrome from '../components/SiteChrome.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const updated = 'August 2, 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — DeftDay Studio"
  description="What DeftDay’s apps store, what they send, and what they never collect. One page, per app, in plain language."
>
  <SiteChrome />
  <div class="sky" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="s1"></span><span class="s2"></span><span class="s3"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <main id="main" class="legal">
    <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
    <h1>What we keep,<br /><span class="serif">and what we don’t.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      DeftDay builds small apps and one browser game. None of them are funded by
      advertising, so none of them are built to watch you. This page says exactly
      what each app stores and what it sends, in plain language.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="studio">
      <h2 id="studio">Across the studio</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not sell or share personal data with data brokers.</li>
        <li>
          Some of our apps carry advertising and some never will. Where an app
          shows ads, it says so in its own section below, and the advertising
          is served by Google AdMob — which uses a resettable advertising ID to
          decide what to show and to count it. Where an app is ad-free, that is
          a promise about that app, not a rule we apply everywhere, and we would
          rather tell you which is which than let one sentence cover both.
        </li>
        <li>
          We do not build advertising profiles ourselves, and we do not follow
          you between our own apps or across the web.
        </li>
        <li>
          Where an app needs to remember something — a preference, a watched
          item, a saved list — it keeps it on your device unless that app’s
          section below says otherwise.
        </li>
        <li>
          This site, <strong>deftday.com</strong>, sets no cookies and stores
          nothing on your device. It counts page views with Google Analytics in
          cookieless mode: Google is sent the page address, the referring link
          and your IP address to register the visit, and nothing is kept that
          could recognise you on a later one — every visit is counted as a
          first. The pages themselves are static and served from Cloudflare,
          which processes request data such as IP address to deliver and
          protect the site.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="munchview">
      <h2 id="munchview">Munchview <span class="tag">Android</span></h2>
      <p>
        Munchview starts a video that fits the time you have. It has no
        analytics and <strong>no advertising, and it will not get any</strong> —
        an app whose whole point is that you are watching within three seconds
        does not get to spend the first one on an ad. Signing in is optional: as
        a guest it collects nothing at all.
      </p>
      <h3>Stored on your device</h3>
      <p>
        Your length preference and what you have already seen, so it does not
        repeat itself. This lives in the app’s own storage and is removed when
        you uninstall the app. It is not sent to us.
      </p>
      <h3>Sent off your device</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A request to our own content service asking for videos, carrying only
          the category or length you chose — not an identifier for you.
        </li>
        <li>
          Video playback happens through YouTube’s player, and thumbnails load
          from YouTube’s servers. Once a video plays, YouTube receives the
          request as it would in any browser and its own privacy policy applies
          (<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">policies.google.com/privacy</a>).
          We do not send YouTube anything about you beyond the video being opened.
        </li>
        <li>
          Some videos come from Dailymotion, Vimeo or Twitch instead, and play
          in that platform’s own embedded player — the card says which one
          before you tap it. Their thumbnails and players are requested the same
          way YouTube’s are, and their own privacy policies apply once open
          (<a href="https://www.dailymotion.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dailymotion</a>,
          <a href="https://vimeo.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vimeo</a>,
          <a href="https://www.twitch.tv/p/legal/privacy-notice/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twitch</a>).
          The searching is done by our content service, so none of them ever see
          a search of yours.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>If you sign in with Google</h3>
      <p>
        Optional, and only there to carry what one phone knows to another. We
        ask Google for your account id, name and email — nothing else. Signing
        in never asks for access to your YouTube account; that is a separate
        choice, described below. Once signed in, the list above is also
        stored by our sync service under your Google account id, so a second
        phone signed in to the same account picks it up. Signing out stops it;
        <b>Delete my account data</b> in the app erases what we hold — or ask us
        at <a href="/munchview/delete-account">deftday.com/munchview/delete-account</a>
        if you no longer have the app installed.
      </p>
      <h3>If you sign in on munchview.app</h3>
      <p>
        The website signs you in with Google itself, separately from the app —
        nothing is typed from one to the other. Our service checks Google's
        credential once and gives the browser a session of its own so you stay
        signed in; that session is signed rather than stored, so there is no
        list of sessions anywhere. The browser then reads the same record
        described above. There is one record and two ways of reading it, and
        nothing is copied between them. Signing out of the browser ends it.
      </p>
      <h3>If you connect your YouTube account</h3>
      <p>
        Separate from signing in, and entirely optional. If you tap
        <b>Connect YouTube</b>, Google asks you to allow one read-only
        permission (<code>youtube.readonly</code>), and we use it for one thing:
        reading the videos you have liked on YouTube so the app can show them as
        their own list. The request goes straight from your phone to YouTube
        using your own permission — your library never passes through our
        servers — and the permission token is not saved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those likes stay a <b>separate list</b>: Munchview's recommendations are
        built from what you like in Munchview, never mixed with what you liked
        on YouTube. We <b>never write anything</b> to your YouTube account — the
        permission we ask for cannot. And we <b>cannot read your YouTube watch
        history</b>: YouTube's API stopped providing it, so no app can. The
        viewing history Munchview shows is the one it recorded on your phone.
        <b>Disconnect</b> removes the imported list from your phone, and you can
        withdraw the permission at any time in your Google account.
      </p>
      <h3>Not collected</h3>
      <p>
        No phone number, contacts, photos, precise location, or advertising
        identifier — signed in or not. Munchview requests no runtime
        permissions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/munchview/privacy">Munchview's full privacy policy<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp;
        <a href="/munchview/delete-account">Delete your account<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp;
        <a href="/gdpr">GDPR notice<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="pickorn">
      <h2 id="pickorn">Pickorn <span class="tag">Android</span></h2>
      <p>
        Pickorn hands you one film for tonight. It is heading for a closed
        Android test track, and this section describes the build testers run.
        It has <strong>no account, no analytics, no advertising and no
        tracking</strong>. Picking a film happens entirely on the phone: the
        catalogue of 33,319 IMDb-rated films ships inside the app, so choosing
        works with no connection at all.
      </p>
      <h3>Stored on your device</h3>
      <p>
        Your watchlist, the films you have marked watched, your mood and
        filter choices, and your language preference. All of it lives in the
        app’s own storage, is never sent anywhere, and is removed when you
        uninstall the app.
      </p>
      <h3>Sent off your device</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Poster images load from IMDb’s image servers
          (<code>m.media-amazon.com</code>). The request carries the poster’s
          address, as it would in any browser — not an identifier for you, and
          nothing about your choices.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tapping the IMDb button opens that film’s IMDb page in your browser,
          where <a href="https://www.imdb.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IMDb’s own privacy policy</a>
          applies.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Not collected</h3>
      <p>
        No phone number, contacts, photos, location, or advertising
        identifier. Pickorn requests no runtime permissions and has nothing to
        sign in to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="unreleased">
      <h2 id="unreleased">Products still in development</h2>
      <p>
        Two products are being built and have not been released. They are not
        named here, and none of them collects anything from anyone today —
        nobody outside the studio is running them. Each gets its own section on
        this page, in full, on the day it reaches a store.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some of them will carry advertising when they are released, and their
        sections will say so plainly on the day they arrive, before anyone can
        install them. Munchview and Pickorn are the ones that will not — that
        is a decision about what they are, and it is written into their
        sections above.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="skybattlesea">
      <h2 id="skybattlesea">SkyBattleSea <span class="tag">Web</span></h2>
      <p>
        SkyBattleSea is playable as a guest or with an account, and it keeps its
        own policy alongside the game:
        <a href="https://skybattlesea.com/privacy.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >skybattlesea.com/privacy.html <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="rights">
      <h2 id="rights">Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        For apps that store everything on your device, uninstalling removes the
        data — we hold nothing to delete. For anything else, or if you simply
        want to ask what we hold, write to
        <a class="mail-inline" href="mailto:hello@deftday.com">hello@deftday.com</a>
        and we will answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Money is on its own page: who took the payment, how to ask that store
        for a refund, and the 14-day right of withdrawal —
        <a href="/refunds">Refunds and withdrawal</a>. What you may do with the
        apps, and when an account is deleted, are in the
        <a href="/terms">Terms of Use</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If this policy changes in a way that affects what we collect, we will
        update the date at the top and describe the change here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back-home"><a href="/">← Back to the studio</a></p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

